00:00:02.659 --> 00:00:05.539 align:start position:0%
today we held an interesting orientation  debate on an amended regulation on the  coordination of social security systems  on the basis of a presidential note  thanks to the cross-border coordination  European citizens can safeguard their  social security entitlement that is  without any doubt an urgent need to  adapt the EU legislation to the user  conferences and to codify the recent  case laws employment opportunities for  instance we see that they are not  distributed evenly across european union  and therefore job seekers needs to be  mobile secondly we want to clarify the  rules on the long-term care benefits how  they are treated in cross-border  situations and which long-term kan care  benefit services may be entitled when  they are moving abroad and thirdly we  address the question of which social  benefits economically inactive citizens  can claim outside their home country let  me be clear free movement does not mean  unconditional free access to social  benefits in other countries inequality  at the labour market does not only have  long lasting social consequences but it  also impacts as I already said on  economic growth with this in mind we  tabled the council conclusions on  enhancing the skills on women and men in  the EU labour market I am glad to recall  that consensus was reached on this text  today the council has the policy debate  on the European semester of the current  here we discussed how to enhance labour  market access and reduce divergences  across the EU there was general  agreement that the situation is  improving in terms of economic growth  and job creation but that there is no  room for complacency we've covered lots  of ground but still a long way to go  this year I'm happy to say that the  implementation of policy reforms through  the European semester is clearly bearing  fruits we see that the employment has  never been so high in Europe  also youth employment and long-term  unemployment are dropping and the number  of people at risk of poverty or social  exclusion has decreased in Europe even  if the level remains high another area  where the European Union is fortunately  making good progress is health and  safety at the workplace we also realize  that small and medium sized enterprises  have sometimes difficulties implementing  these standards by addressing  occupational safety and health in SMS we  have an opportunity to make a real  difference for millions of workers  because we all know SMEs are the biggest  employees in Europe
 

